A||||6||jackson||Clutch slave rod - lengthen||||1||
Z||000000||jackson||04-11-2001||04:40 PM||||I've heard of others who have lengthened the rod that actuates the clutch in order to get the release point up off the floor. I just put in a lengthened rod and it does do the trick. However, will this create pressure on the throw-out bearing thus causing it to spin at a high rate all the time and wear out?||||reg||1
Z||000001||jackson||04-11-2001||04:41 PM||||By the way, this is on an 88 Formula V6.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tryxalon||04-11-2001||06:12 PM||||yes, you have just insured failure of both your slave cylinder and your throwout bearing by changing the geometry of the mechanism. <P>Do a search on Slave Cylinder or Clutch.  Much discussion on this subject. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||04-11-2001||06:16 PM||||Unless you go to extremes - the length of the slave cyl rod has absoultely no effect on the clutch. Due to the spring in the slave cyl the TO bearing is always touching the pressure plate||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||04-11-2001||06:19 PM||||GM doesn't just pull these measurements out of their a$$. They are a certain length for a reason.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Philphine||04-11-2001||06:23 PM||||i did it. i worked up a threaded rod so i could adjust it when i was having a clutch problem. i finally has to have it repaired and they told me it's a nonadjustable system i'd done more harm than good. had to find my old rod and relace the lever i drilled through to make it all original again.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tryxalon||04-11-2001||06:35 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007584-2.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007584-2.html</A>  <P>My solution to my "clutch problem".  Course mine was with a Isuzu<BR> 5-speed tranny on an Iron Duke ... don't know that the same applies to yours.<P>This stuff is not rocket science, but it is physics and mechanical engineering. Or "Bodging" as my Brit friends used to call it.   <BR>||||reg||1
